# Design: end-to-end freshness harness as a parameterized test

## Problem
The performance plan's north-star metric (append on a device to
visibility through the remote sql tool) had no harness. Polling a
remote hub from a test is easy to get subtly wrong: subtracting
timestamps across hosts, querying events_settled (which hides the
last ten minutes by design), waiting for visibility before the next
append (which hides queue growth), and dropping timed-out samples
(which flatters percentiles).

## Why This Way
One process plays device and observer: the appender writes the JSONL
and the observer polls the remote sql tool, so the lag is one
monotonic clock subtraction, never cross-host wall clock. Markers are
`ohyafresh-<run>-<seq>` in the record text, matched with a LIKE on
the direct events table, paged by `id > last` so the tool's 500-row
bound never truncates a drain. The appender runs at a fixed rate with
a random phase offset against the upload interval plus jitter
(reproducible through OHYA_FRESH_SEED) and never waits for
visibility. Timed-out samples keep drain-window lower-bound lags in
the distribution; a bounded health wait after the drain reports late
deliveries and records that never landed, without touching the
percentiles. The observer warms its first exchange before the first
append so a cold tsnet dial (seconds) cannot poison sample one.

## Key Trade-offs
- Polling bounds visibility from above; the report carries the poll
  resolution and query durations instead of pretending precision.
- The in-test run stays at ~100 samples on the shipped 2 s interval:
  a smoke check, not p99 evidence; T070 scales it with env knobs.
- The stack sits on one host (loopback tailnet path); the DERP relay
  leg stays out of scope with blocked T065.

